      <div>
        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of the Hydaspes</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">May 326 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The banks of the Hydaspes (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>) river, in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, with the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>; the Pauravas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>. Of the Macedonian army, an estimated 40,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry crossed the river in time to fight; the Indian infantry outnumbered the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> 5 to 1</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> won and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>. Large areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> were absorbed into the Macedonian Empire, and Alexander reinstated Porus as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
