    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Alexander crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>-swollen river about 27 km upstream of his camp, using feints and deception to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> from learning where and when.</li><li>The Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> did heavy damage to the Macedonian foot, but they were driven back and trampled many of their own side, and the Macedonian cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> then closed on the Indian army from both sides.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> gives Macedonian losses of 80 foot soldiers, ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a>, twenty Companions and 200 other horsemen, and Indian losses of 23,000; Diodorus has 12,000 Indians dead and over 9,000 captured, and J. F. C. Fuller thought his figure of 1,000 Macedonian dead more realistic.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> rode his tallest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephant</a> into the battle, against the usual custom of Indian kings of fighting from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>, and the animal carried no howdah because the king wore chain mail.</p></aside>
    </section>

