    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">328 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander defeats the last Achaemenid forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> and Spitamenes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">327 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander fortifies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> with 10,000 men and invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander allies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> and camps on the north bank of the Hydaspes</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander crosses the river upstream by night in a storm and routs a force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>' son</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Macedonian cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> defeat the Indian cavalry and elephants and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> is captured</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nikaia</a> and Boukephala and leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> his lands</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Alexander's army refuses to go further at the Hyphasis, and he marches south along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-hydaspes" data-v="9e4cec735d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies draw up</b> The Indians stood with cavalry on both flanks, chariots in front and infantry in the centre with elephants before it. Alexander judged the centre the strongest part and kept his phalanx back while his cavalry prepared to go for the flanks.</li><li><b>The Macedonian cavalry strikes the Indian flanks</b> The Dahae horse archers harassed the Indian right-wing cavalry, and the Companions, with Alexander leading, charged the Indian cavalry on the left wing, which was outnumbered.</li><li><b>The Indian right-wing cavalry is caught from behind</b> The rest of the Indian cavalry rode over to help the hard-pressed left wing. Coenus's squadrons followed and struck them from the rear, so the Indian horsemen had to face two attacks at once.</li><li><b>The Indian cavalry is routed</b> The Indian horsemen tried to form a double phalanx, but the manoeuvre added to their confusion. They were routed and fled towards the safety of their elephants.</li><li><b>The elephants advance and meet the phalanx</b> The war elephants advanced and were met by the Macedonian phalanx. They caused heavy losses among the Macedonian foot, trampling and disorganising their dense lines, but the infantry kept up its resistance.</li><li><b>The elephants are driven back into their own lines</b> The elephants were repulsed and fled back to their own lines. Many of their mahouts had been struck down, and the maddened animals trampled many of their own infantry and cavalry.</li><li><b>The phalanx advances and the cavalry charges from behind</b> The Macedonian foot locked their shields and advanced on the confused enemy mass while the cavalry charged from the rear. The whole Indian army was routed, and Craterus's force, having crossed the river, pursued the fleeing Indians.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/porus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porus.jpg" alt="Porus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porus_alexander_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">User PHGCOM, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After defeating the last Achaemenid forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> and Spitamenes in 328 BCE, Alexander began a new campaign in 327 BCE to extend his empire towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> with 10,000 men and entered India through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a>. A smaller force under his own command took the northern route and the fortress of Aornos, a place of mythological significance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Herakles</a> was said to have failed to occupy it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> gave his army the toughest opposition it had faced, and he won although outnumbered, by between 3 to 1 and 5 to 1 depending on the source.</p><p>In early spring of the next year Alexander allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Taxiles</a> (local name Ambhi), king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, and they combined their forces against Taxiles' neighbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> the Elder, king of the Hydaspes. Porus had refused Alexander's order to surrender and was preparing for war. Alexander had to subdue him to keep marching east: a strong opponent left on his flank would endanger further campaigns, and he could not show weakness if he wanted to keep the loyalty of the Indian princes already subdued. Porus in turn had to defend his kingdom and chose a place well suited to checking the advance.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Hydaspes&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Peter Green, Alexander of Macedon, 356–323 B.C.: A Historical Biography (University of California Press, 1991)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrian.jpg" alt="Arrian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrian7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WikiSysop, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Macedonian army was much the larger. An estimated 40,000 infantry and 5,000 cavalry crossed the river in time to fight. The detachment that crossed with Alexander consisted, according to the 2nd century CE Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, of 6,000 on foot and 5,000 on horseback, though it was probably larger. Craterus stayed in the camp with most of the army, and Meleager, Attalus and Gorgias commanded other forces. Alexander had Dahae <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a>, the armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> and the heavy infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> with its long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissas</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> drew up with cavalry on both flanks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> in front, and in the centre infantry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> stationed every fifty feet before them to deter the Macedonian cavalry. The elephants were heavily armoured and carried howdahs with three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and javelin men. Porus' soldiers wore bright clothes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> helmets and carried axes, lances and maces. The Indian infantry outnumbered the phalanx by 5 to 1 but lacked armour and was at a disadvantage at close quarters against the sarissas. Alexander saw that the Indian line was strongest in the centre and planned to attack the flanks with his cavalry first, keeping the phalanx back until the Indian cavalry had been neutralised.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander camped on the north bank of the Hydaspes, which was deep and fast enough that a direct crossing would probably doom the attackers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> shadowed Alexander's cavalry as it moved up and down the bank each night. Alexander used feints, detours and, it was reported, a look-alike in a mock royal tent to lull Porus. Alexander crossed about 27 km upstream, where a wooded island divided the river, on skin floats filled with hay and on boats cut in half. He first landed on the island by mistake. A storm that night drowned out the noise.</p><p>Porus sent his son with a small force of cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> against the landing. Alexander's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> showered it with arrows, his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> charged at once, and the chariots were stuck in the mud. The younger Porus died and his force was routed. The elder Porus left a detachment opposite Craterus and marched against Alexander. The Dahae harassed the Indian right and Alexander led the Companions against the left. The rest of the Indian cavalry came to help, Coenus attacked it from the rear, and it fled to the elephants.</p><p>The elephants advanced and caused heavy losses among the Macedonian foot, but the infantry resisted with javelins and axes, and the elephants were driven back and trampled many of their own side. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> locked shields and advanced while the cavalry charged from the rear, and the Indian army broke. Craterus crossed and joined the pursuit. Alexander is said to have admired Porus' courage and sent several messengers before Meroes, a friend of Porus, persuaded the thirsty king to dismount and be taken to him.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Hydaspes&#8221; cites where it covers Pre-battle manoeuvres, Battle:</p><ul><li>Kaushik Roy, India's Historic Battles: From Alexander the Great to Kargil (Orient Blackswan, 2004)</li><li>John M. Kistler, War Elephants (Greenwood Publishing Group, 2006)</li><li>K. A. Nilakanta Sastri (ed.), Age of the Nandas and Mauryas (Motilal Banarsidass, 1988)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, Macedonian losses were 80 foot soldiers, ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a>, twenty Companions and 200 other horsemen. J. F. C. Fuller thought Diodorus' report of 1,000 killed more realistic. Indian losses were 23,000 according to Arrian and over 12,000 dead and over 9,000 captured according to Diodorus. Two sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>, his relative and ally Spitakes and most of his chieftains were killed. Around 80 elephants were captured alive, and Alexander gained 70 more when reinforcements called by Porus arrived late and surrendered.</p><p>Asked how he wished to be treated, Porus replied "Treat me as a king would treat another king". Alexander let him keep his lands. He founded two cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nikaia</a> at the site of the battle, named after the Greek word for victory, and Boukephala on the opposite bank, for his horse Bucephalus, who died during or after the battle.</p><p>Alexander then marched east towards the Nanda Empire but stopped at the Hyphasis (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a>) in 326 BCE. His army, tired from nearly eight years of campaigning and worried about facing another very large Indian army, refused to go on. Historians do not call this a mutiny but a rise in military unrest. Alexander went south along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> instead, securing its banks as the borders of his empire, and left Porus in charge of the easternmost territory.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Hydaspes&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath and legacy:</p><ul><li>Kaushik Roy, India's Historic Battles: From Alexander the Great to Kargil (Orient Blackswan, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known mainly through Greek historians, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, who wrote in the 2nd century CE, and Diodorus. They differ on losses, and the totals for the Indian dead given by the two are close if Arrian counted prisoners in his figure. Fuller preferred Diodorus' Macedonian figure of 1,000 dead to Arrian's much smaller one.</p><p>Some details rest on report alone: the look-alike in the royal tent, and Alexander's growing admiration for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a>. Arrian's figure of 6,000 foot and 5,000 horse for the crossing force is thought probably too small. Peter Green judged that Porus' performance outclassed that of Memnon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and of Spitamenes. Greek historians agree that Porus fought bravely to the end.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Alexander's crossing in the face of the Indian army, despite close watch by the Pauravas, has been called one of his masterpieces in combat. The battle is counted a very significant event in the Wars of Alexander, because it exposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> to Greek political and cultural influences that would affect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and Indians for centuries. Large areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>.</p><p>The main reasons for Porus' defeat were Alexander's tactics and the Macedonians' discipline and technology. The Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> were inferior to the Greek cavalry, the Indians had no well supported military infrastructure or standing army, and Porus mainly tried to counter his opponent's moves instead of taking the initiative. The Hydaspes was the hardest fought of Alexander's engagements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in what was possibly his most costly engagement Porus became his most successful recorded opponent.</p><p>Under the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> the tactician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Kautilya</a> took the battle as a lesson and stressed military training before battle. The first Mauryan emperor, Chandragupta, kept a standing army, and the chariot corps played a marginal part in Mauryan military organisation.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Hydaspes&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath and legacy:</p><ul><li>Kaushik Roy, India's Historic Battles: From Alexander the Great to Kargil (Orient Blackswan, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In May 326 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>-swollen Hydaspes in secret, defeated the Pauravas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> and captured him. Porus was kept as a ruler under Alexander, and the army turned back at the Hyphasis.</em></p>

